To add the two new databases with minimal changes to your existing folder structure, the standard approach is to split your **Entities** and **Repositories** into dedicated sub-packages for each database.

Because JPA needs a distinct `EntityManagerFactory` for each physical database, grouping your classes by folder is the cleanest and most maintainable way to route transactions.

---

### Step 1: Update `application.properties`

Replace your generic `spring.datasource.*` lines with three separate blocks. Notice the custom Hikari settings added to each:

```properties
# ---------------- DATABASE 1 (Primary) ----------------
app.datasource.db1.jdbc-url=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/db_one
app.datasource.db1.username=user1
app.datasource.db1.password=pass1
app.datasource.db1.driver-class-name=org.postgresql.Driver
app.datasource.db1.hikari.pool-name=Db1Pool
app.datasource.db1.hikari.maximum-pool-size=10

# ---------------- DATABASE 2 (Secondary) --------------
app.datasource.db2.jdbc-url=jdbc:postgresql://remote-host-2:5432/db_two
app.datasource.db2.username=user2
app.datasource.db2.password=pass2
app.datasource.db2.driver-class-name=org.postgresql.Driver
app.datasource.db2.hikari.pool-name=Db2Pool
app.datasource.db2.hikari.maximum-pool-size=10

# ---------------- DATABASE 3 (Tertiary) ---------------
app.datasource.db3.jdbc-url=jdbc:postgresql://remote-host-3:5432/db_three
app.datasource.db3.username=user3
app.datasource.db3.password=pass3
app.datasource.db3.driver-class-name=org.postgresql.Driver
app.datasource.db3.hikari.pool-name=Db3Pool
app.datasource.db3.hikari.maximum-pool-size=10

```

---

### Step 2: Minimal Folder Structure Update

Organize your entities and repositories into three sub-packages under your main package:

```text
com.example.myapp
├── config
│   ├── Db1Config.java
│   ├── Db2Config.java
│   └── Db3Config.java
├── db1
│   ├── UserEntity.java
│   └── UserRepository.java
├── db2
│   ├── OrderEntity.java
│   └── OrderRepository.java
└── db3
    ├── PaymentEntity.java
    └── PaymentRepository.java

```

---

### Step 3: Java Configuration Classes

#### Database 1 Configuration (`Db1Config.java`)

Mark this one with `@Primary` so Spring knows it is your default database.

```java
package com.example.myapp.config;

import com.zaxxer.hikari.HikariDataSource;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Qualifier;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.jdbc.DataSourceProperties;
import org.springframework.boot.context.properties.ConfigurationProperties;
import org.springframework.boot.orm.jpa.EntityManagerFactoryBuilder;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Primary;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.config.EnableJpaRepositories;
import org.springframework.orm.jpa.JpaTransactionManager;
import org.springframework.orm.jpa.LocalContainerEntityManagerFactoryBean;
import org.springframework.transaction.PlatformTransactionManager;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.EnableTransactionManagement;

import javax.sql.DataSource;
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManagerFactory;

@Configuration
@EnableTransactionManagement
@EnableJpaRepositories(
    basePackages = "com.example.myapp.db1",
    entityManagerFactoryRef = "db1EntityManagerFactory",
    transactionManagerRef = "db1TransactionManager"
)
public class Db1Config {

    @Primary
    @Bean(name = "db1Properties")
    @ConfigurationProperties("app.datasource.db1")
    public DataSourceProperties dataSourceProperties() {
        return new DataSourceProperties();
    }

    @Primary
    @Bean(name = "db1DataSource")
    @ConfigurationProperties("app.datasource.db1.hikari")
    public DataSource dataSource(@Qualifier("db1Properties") DataSourceProperties properties) {
        return properties.initializeDataSourceBuilder().type(HikariDataSource.class).build();
    }

    @Primary
    @Bean(name = "db1EntityManagerFactory")
    public LocalContainerEntityManagerFactoryBean entityManagerFactory(
            EntityManagerFactoryBuilder builder,
            @Qualifier("db1DataSource") DataSource dataSource) {
        return builder
                .dataSource(dataSource)
                .packages("com.example.myapp.db1")
                .persistenceUnit("db1")
                .build();
    }

    @Primary
    @Bean(name = "db1TransactionManager")
    public PlatformTransactionManager transactionManager(
            @Qualifier("db1EntityManagerFactory") EntityManagerFactory entityManagerFactory) {
        return new JpaTransactionManager(entityManagerFactory);
    }
}

```

#### Database 2 Configuration (`Db2Config.java`)

This configuration is identical to Database 1, but without the `@Primary` annotations:

```java
package com.example.myapp.config;

import com.zaxxer.hikari.HikariDataSource;
import org.springframework.beans.factory.annotation.Qualifier;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.jdbc.DataSourceProperties;
import org.springframework.boot.context.properties.ConfigurationProperties;
import org.springframework.boot.orm.jpa.EntityManagerFactoryBuilder;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.config.EnableJpaRepositories;
import org.springframework.orm.jpa.JpaTransactionManager;
import org.springframework.orm.jpa.LocalContainerEntityManagerFactoryBean;
import org.springframework.transaction.PlatformTransactionManager;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.EnableTransactionManagement;

import javax.sql.DataSource;
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManagerFactory;

@Configuration
@EnableTransactionManagement
@EnableJpaRepositories(
    basePackages = "com.example.myapp.db2",
    entityManagerFactoryRef = "db2EntityManagerFactory",
    transactionManagerRef = "db2TransactionManager"
)
public class Db2Config {

    @Bean(name = "db2Properties")
    @ConfigurationProperties("app.datasource.db2")
    public DataSourceProperties dataSourceProperties() {
        return new DataSourceProperties();
    }

    @Bean(name = "db2DataSource")
    @ConfigurationProperties("app.datasource.db2.hikari")
    public DataSource dataSource(@Qualifier("db2Properties") DataSourceProperties properties) {
        return properties.initializeDataSourceBuilder().type(HikariDataSource.class).build();
    }

    @Bean(name = "db2EntityManagerFactory")
    public LocalContainerEntityManagerFactoryBean entityManagerFactory(
            EntityManagerFactoryBuilder builder,
            @Qualifier("db2DataSource") DataSource dataSource) {
        return builder
                .dataSource(dataSource)
                .packages("com.example.myapp.db2")
                .persistenceUnit("db2")
                .build();
    }

    @Bean(name = "db2TransactionManager")
    public PlatformTransactionManager transactionManager(
            @Qualifier("db2EntityManagerFactory") EntityManagerFactory entityManagerFactory) {
        return new JpaTransactionManager(entityManagerFactory);
    }
}

```

#### Database 3 Configuration (`Db3Config.java`)

Create this file by copying `Db2Config.java`, replacing every occurrence of `db2` with `db3` (e.g., package to `com.example.myapp.db3`, properties to `app.datasource.db3`, etc.).

---

### Step 4: Using Repositories and Transactions

Your Spring Service classes will work normally via standard dependency injection:

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {

    private final UserRepository userRepo;       // Automatically connects to DB1 pool
    private final OrderRepository orderRepo;     // Automatically connects to DB2 pool

    public OrderService(UserRepository userRepo, OrderRepository orderRepo) {
        this.userRepo = userRepo;
        this.orderRepo = orderRepo;
    }

    // Explicitly declare which transaction manager handles the transaction
    @Transactional("db2TransactionManager")
    public void createOrder(OrderEntity order) {
        orderRepo.save(order);
    }
}

```

> **Note:** If your Spring Boot version is older (Spring Boot 2.x), replace the imports containing `jakarta.persistence.*` with `javax.persistence.*`.

Are your databases hosted on the same engine (e.g., all PostgreSQL), or will any of them require different SQL dialects and drivers?
